1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,651
How can a garden in the middle

2
00:00:02,651 --> 00:00:04,697
of one city be worth as much

3
00:00:04,697 --> 00:00:07,598
as an entire American state?

4
00:00:07,850 --> 00:00:08,765
In Tokyo,

5
00:00:08,765 --> 00:00:11,321
at the end of the nineteen eighties,

6
00:00:11,321 --> 00:00:12,270
it was.

7
00:00:12,270 --> 00:00:14,055
At neighbouring land prices,

8
00:00:14,055 --> 00:00:16,991
economists estimated that the land under

9
00:00:16,991 --> 00:00:19,250
the Emperor's palace was worth as much

10
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:21,035
as all of California,

11
00:00:21,035 --> 00:00:22,990
or all of Canada.

12
00:00:23,150 --> 00:00:25,475
Japan's land as a whole was worth

13
00:00:25,475 --> 00:00:27,800
about four times all the land in

14
00:00:27,800 --> 00:00:29,630
the United States.

15
00:00:29,630 --> 00:00:31,156
Then it all came down,

16
00:00:31,156 --> 00:00:33,484
and Japan's stock market would not see

17
00:00:33,484 --> 00:00:35,812
its old record again for more than

18
00:00:35,812 --> 00:00:37,446
thirty-four years.

19
00:00:48,083 --> 00:00:52,086
Where did all that money come from?

20
00:00:57,083 --> 00:00:59,483
Land in Japan had been a one-way

21
00:00:59,483 --> 00:01:01,283
bet for decades.

22
00:01:01,373 --> 00:01:04,176
Between nineteen fifty-five and nineteen

23
00:01:04,176 --> 00:01:05,087
ninety,

24
00:01:05,087 --> 00:01:07,190
land in the six largest cities rose

25
00:01:07,190 --> 00:01:10,593
twenty times faster than consumer prices.

26
00:01:10,693 --> 00:01:11,596
Nationwide,

27
00:01:11,596 --> 00:01:14,145
land gained about fifteen times its value

28
00:01:14,145 --> 00:01:16,925
between nineteen fifty-five and nineteen

29
00:01:16,925 --> 00:01:18,683
seventy alone.

30
00:01:18,803 --> 00:01:21,192
And from around nineteen eighty-three,

31
00:01:21,192 --> 00:01:23,604
prices in central Tokyo climbed faster

32
00:01:23,604 --> 00:01:24,020
still,

33
00:01:24,020 --> 00:01:26,651
as foreign financial firms rushed to open

34
00:01:26,651 --> 00:01:28,786
offices in the city.

35
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:31,360
It started with a deal.

36
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:33,766
In September nineteen eighty-five,

37
00:01:33,766 --> 00:01:34,802
the Group of Five,

38
00:01:34,802 --> 00:01:37,108
the leading industrial economies,

39
00:01:37,108 --> 00:01:39,223
agreed to push the dollar down against

40
00:01:39,223 --> 00:01:42,150
the currencies of Japan and Germany.

41
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:45,260
It became known as the Plaza Accord.

42
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:47,076
Japan's currency soared,

43
00:01:47,076 --> 00:01:49,805
and the country feared a recession caused

44
00:01:49,805 --> 00:01:50,860
by it.

45
00:01:50,920 --> 00:01:53,932
So the central bank began cutting interest

46
00:01:53,932 --> 00:01:54,660
rates,

47
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:56,516
again and again.

48
00:01:56,750 --> 00:01:59,433
By February nineteen eighty-seven,

49
00:01:59,433 --> 00:02:01,913
the official interest rate was two and

50
00:02:01,913 --> 00:02:03,017
a half percent,

51
00:02:03,017 --> 00:02:05,249
the lowest in Japan's history,

52
00:02:05,249 --> 00:02:07,504
and it stayed there until May nineteen

53
00:02:07,504 --> 00:02:08,780
eighty-nine.

54
00:02:09,110 --> 00:02:11,288
Companies had more cash than they could

55
00:02:11,288 --> 00:02:12,130
use.

56
00:02:12,190 --> 00:02:14,530
Japan's Ministry of Finance says fund

57
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:17,572
management became so fashionable that a

58
00:02:17,572 --> 00:02:18,953
new word appeared,

59
00:02:18,953 --> 00:02:19,632
zaitech,

60
00:02:19,632 --> 00:02:21,870
financial engineering.

61
00:02:22,020 --> 00:02:24,399
Companies started making money from

62
00:02:24,399 --> 00:02:26,040
trading shares and land,

63
00:02:26,040 --> 00:02:28,067
not just from making things.

64
00:02:28,317 --> 00:02:30,562
Let me draw the machine that turned

65
00:02:30,562 --> 00:02:33,157
cheap money into a bubble.

66
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:35,427
A company owns land.

67
00:02:35,427 --> 00:02:38,017
The bank lends against that land.

68
00:02:38,027 --> 00:02:40,574
The company uses the loan to buy

69
00:02:40,574 --> 00:02:41,313
more land,

70
00:02:41,313 --> 00:02:42,677
or shares.

71
00:02:42,717 --> 00:02:44,767
That pushes prices up.

72
00:02:44,767 --> 00:02:47,374
Higher prices make the land worth more,

73
00:02:47,374 --> 00:02:49,697
so the bank lends even more.

74
00:02:49,697 --> 00:02:51,417
Round and round.

75
00:02:51,417 --> 00:02:54,262
Each turn makes everyone feel richer,

76
00:02:54,262 --> 00:02:56,950
and makes the next loan look safe.

77
00:02:57,183 --> 00:02:59,983
The central bank saw the danger.

78
00:03:00,193 --> 00:03:02,554
The Ministry of Finance history quotes a

79
00:03:02,554 --> 00:03:04,243
saying used at the time.

80
00:03:04,383 --> 00:03:07,083
Dry firewood piled up goes up in

81
00:03:07,083 --> 00:03:08,203
flames.

82
00:03:08,203 --> 00:03:10,643
The money was the firewood.

83
00:03:10,753 --> 00:03:12,919
But ordinary prices in the shops were

84
00:03:12,919 --> 00:03:13,490
stable,

85
00:03:13,490 --> 00:03:15,262
so there was no obvious reason to

86
00:03:15,262 --> 00:03:17,033
raise interest rates.

87
00:03:17,033 --> 00:03:19,988
And the pile kept growing.

88
00:03:20,250 --> 00:03:22,326
By the end of the decade,

89
00:03:22,326 --> 00:03:25,292
the numbers had stopped making sense.

90
00:03:30,950 --> 00:03:34,539
How expensive did Tokyo get?

91
00:03:39,950 --> 00:03:41,970
In Tokyo's Ginza district,

92
00:03:41,970 --> 00:03:43,735
one square meter of land,

93
00:03:43,735 --> 00:03:46,267
about the size of a small table,

94
00:03:46,267 --> 00:03:48,569
was worth more than two hundred thousand

95
00:03:48,569 --> 00:03:49,680
dollars.

96
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:51,593
Choice land downtown sold for the

97
00:03:51,593 --> 00:03:54,534
equivalent of nearly a billion dollars an

98
00:03:54,534 --> 00:03:55,360
acre.

99
00:03:55,650 --> 00:03:57,883
Office space in the financial district

100
00:03:57,883 --> 00:04:00,340
cost nearly seventy-five thousand dollars

101
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:01,610
per square foot.

102
00:04:01,890 --> 00:04:04,512
Even simple houses rented for more than

103
00:04:04,512 --> 00:04:06,780
ten thousand dollars a month.

104
00:04:07,017 --> 00:04:08,422
Add it all up,

105
00:04:08,422 --> 00:04:11,097
and the numbers become absurd.

106
00:04:11,097 --> 00:04:13,800
By the end of nineteen eighty-nine,

107
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:15,783
all the land in Japan was worth

108
00:04:15,783 --> 00:04:18,367
about fifteen trillion dollars.

109
00:04:18,397 --> 00:04:20,052
In just four years,

110
00:04:20,052 --> 00:04:23,015
its value had more than doubled.

111
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:26,030
The banks were deep inside it.

112
00:04:26,030 --> 00:04:28,930
When the Ministry of Finance inspected

113
00:04:28,930 --> 00:04:29,431
them,

114
00:04:29,431 --> 00:04:31,541
it found that loans linked to land

115
00:04:31,541 --> 00:04:34,178
and to zaitech had significantly

116
00:04:34,178 --> 00:04:35,206
increased,

117
00:04:35,206 --> 00:04:37,052
and that the checks on those land

118
00:04:37,052 --> 00:04:38,950
loans were incomplete.

119
00:04:38,950 --> 00:04:41,575
Land was the collateral behind almost

120
00:04:41,575 --> 00:04:42,744
everything,

121
00:04:42,744 --> 00:04:44,653
and its price was the one number

122
00:04:44,653 --> 00:04:47,162
nobody wanted to question.

123
00:04:47,417 --> 00:04:49,697
Let me draw what that meant.

124
00:04:49,697 --> 00:04:51,444
Here is the United States,

125
00:04:51,444 --> 00:04:53,408
with about twenty-five times more land

126
00:04:53,408 --> 00:04:54,597
than Japan.

127
00:04:54,967 --> 00:04:56,390
Here is the value of all that

128
00:04:56,390 --> 00:04:58,007
American land.

129
00:04:58,147 --> 00:04:59,925
And here is the value of Japan's

130
00:04:59,925 --> 00:05:00,747
land.

131
00:05:01,067 --> 00:05:03,267
About four times bigger.

132
00:05:03,537 --> 00:05:05,413
An average acre in Japan was worth

133
00:05:05,413 --> 00:05:07,477
a hundred times an average acre in

134
00:05:07,477 --> 00:05:08,827
America.

135
00:05:09,147 --> 00:05:10,386
By one estimate,

136
00:05:10,386 --> 00:05:12,275
Japan held about half of the value

137
00:05:12,275 --> 00:05:14,345
of all the land in the world.

138
00:05:14,583 --> 00:05:16,543
And then there is the most famous

139
00:05:16,543 --> 00:05:18,573
comparison of all.

140
00:05:18,573 --> 00:05:20,670
The Imperial Palace sits in the heart

141
00:05:20,670 --> 00:05:21,487
of Tokyo,

142
00:05:21,487 --> 00:05:23,584
a walled garden in the middle of

143
00:05:23,584 --> 00:05:24,813
the city.

144
00:05:25,133 --> 00:05:26,796
Valued at the price of the land

145
00:05:26,796 --> 00:05:27,606
around it,

146
00:05:27,606 --> 00:05:30,308
economists estimated it was worth as much

147
00:05:30,308 --> 00:05:32,313
as all of California.

148
00:05:32,313 --> 00:05:34,153
It was never for sale.

149
00:05:34,153 --> 00:05:35,528
But on paper,

150
00:05:35,528 --> 00:05:38,093
one garden could buy a state.

151
00:05:38,350 --> 00:05:40,907
And on the last trading day of

152
00:05:40,907 --> 00:05:43,112
nineteen eighty-nine,

153
00:05:43,112 --> 00:05:45,949
the stock market hit its peak.

154
00:05:51,583 --> 00:05:54,757
What pops a bubble this big?

155
00:06:00,583 --> 00:06:01,883
Interest rates.

156
00:06:01,883 --> 00:06:03,676
In May nineteen eighty-nine,

157
00:06:03,676 --> 00:06:05,892
the central bank finally began to raise

158
00:06:05,892 --> 00:06:06,633
them.

159
00:06:06,633 --> 00:06:08,293
Then again in October,

160
00:06:08,293 --> 00:06:10,363
and again on Christmas Day.

161
00:06:10,583 --> 00:06:13,220
In March nineteen ninety came another

162
00:06:13,220 --> 00:06:13,638
rise,

163
00:06:13,638 --> 00:06:14,715
and in August,

164
00:06:14,715 --> 00:06:16,793
the rate reached six percent.

165
00:06:16,793 --> 00:06:18,445
In just over a year,

166
00:06:18,445 --> 00:06:20,361
the price of money had more than

167
00:06:20,361 --> 00:06:21,439
doubled.

168
00:06:21,683 --> 00:06:24,013
Some saw it coming.

169
00:06:24,083 --> 00:06:25,778
One of the paper's authors,

170
00:06:25,778 --> 00:06:27,902
the economist William Ziemba,

171
00:06:27,902 --> 00:06:29,619
was then working with the research

172
00:06:29,619 --> 00:06:33,223
institute of Yamaichi Securities in Tokyo.

173
00:06:33,603 --> 00:06:34,551
His model,

174
00:06:34,551 --> 00:06:36,737
comparing bond yields with company

175
00:06:36,737 --> 00:06:37,442
earnings,

176
00:06:37,442 --> 00:06:40,601
flashed a danger signal in April nineteen

177
00:06:40,601 --> 00:06:41,548
eighty-nine,

178
00:06:41,548 --> 00:06:43,363
months before the peak.

179
00:06:43,543 --> 00:06:45,847
It could say a crash was coming,

180
00:06:45,847 --> 00:06:47,223
but not when.

181
00:06:47,353 --> 00:06:49,633
And Yamaichi's top management,

182
00:06:49,633 --> 00:06:50,531
he writes,

183
00:06:50,531 --> 00:06:52,052
did not listen.

184
00:06:52,317 --> 00:06:54,897
The stock market turned first.

185
00:06:55,147 --> 00:06:57,479
On the last trading day of nineteen

186
00:06:57,479 --> 00:06:58,389
eighty-nine,

187
00:06:58,389 --> 00:07:00,721
the Nikkei index closed near thirty-eight

188
00:07:00,721 --> 00:07:02,487
thousand nine hundred.

189
00:07:02,757 --> 00:07:04,567
From the very first trading day of

190
00:07:04,567 --> 00:07:05,955
nineteen ninety,

191
00:07:05,955 --> 00:07:06,957
it fell.

192
00:07:07,017 --> 00:07:08,315
By October,

193
00:07:08,315 --> 00:07:10,700
it had lost forty-eight percent of its

194
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:11,565
value.

195
00:07:11,817 --> 00:07:14,521
Even famous investors got the timing

196
00:07:14,521 --> 00:07:15,367
wrong.

197
00:07:15,377 --> 00:07:17,443
According to the same paper,

198
00:07:17,443 --> 00:07:19,765
George Soros bet against the Japanese

199
00:07:19,765 --> 00:07:21,135
market too early,

200
00:07:21,135 --> 00:07:22,737
in nineteen eighty-eight,

201
00:07:22,737 --> 00:07:25,427
and lost about one billion dollars.

202
00:07:25,657 --> 00:07:28,132
A bubble can stay irrational longer than

203
00:07:28,132 --> 00:07:30,382
most people can afford to wait.

204
00:07:30,617 --> 00:07:33,397
Land held on a little longer.

205
00:07:33,587 --> 00:07:35,133
Even as shares fell,

206
00:07:35,133 --> 00:07:38,197
the public stayed bullish about property.

207
00:07:38,507 --> 00:07:40,746
So in March nineteen ninety,

208
00:07:40,746 --> 00:07:43,008
the Ministry of Finance stepped in

209
00:07:43,008 --> 00:07:44,287
directly.

210
00:07:44,617 --> 00:07:46,404
It told banks to keep their real

211
00:07:46,404 --> 00:07:48,701
estate loans from growing faster than

212
00:07:48,701 --> 00:07:50,577
their total lending.

213
00:07:50,897 --> 00:07:53,273
The flow of new money into land

214
00:07:53,273 --> 00:07:54,665
was cut off.

215
00:07:54,917 --> 00:07:57,297
First the shares fell.

216
00:07:57,337 --> 00:07:59,287
Then the golf clubs.

217
00:07:59,287 --> 00:08:00,597
And finally,

218
00:08:00,597 --> 00:08:01,731
the land.

219
00:08:07,383 --> 00:08:10,277
What happens to a country when its

220
00:08:10,277 --> 00:08:12,163
land stops going up?

221
00:08:16,383 --> 00:08:19,703
The strangest signal came from golf.

222
00:08:19,873 --> 00:08:20,936
In the bubble,

223
00:08:20,936 --> 00:08:23,106
memberships of golf clubs were traded like

224
00:08:23,106 --> 00:08:23,735
shares,

225
00:08:23,735 --> 00:08:25,883
and according to the researchers,

226
00:08:25,883 --> 00:08:28,269
that market was an even bigger bubble

227
00:08:28,269 --> 00:08:29,303
than stocks.

228
00:08:29,703 --> 00:08:31,963
It fell after the stock market,

229
00:08:31,963 --> 00:08:33,833
and before the land.

230
00:08:33,833 --> 00:08:34,324
Then,

231
00:08:34,324 --> 00:08:36,365
from nineteen ninety-one,

232
00:08:36,365 --> 00:08:39,291
land prices began a long decline.

233
00:08:39,550 --> 00:08:41,891
Let me draw why this hurt for

234
00:08:41,891 --> 00:08:43,010
so long.

235
00:08:43,010 --> 00:08:45,221
Here is the loop from before,

236
00:08:45,221 --> 00:08:46,940
running backwards.

237
00:08:47,050 --> 00:08:48,940
Land prices fall.

238
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:50,298
So the land that backs the loans

239
00:08:50,298 --> 00:08:51,100
is worth less.

240
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:54,101
The banks are left with bad loans,

241
00:08:54,101 --> 00:08:56,600
and they stop lending.

242
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:58,740
Companies cannot borrow,

243
00:08:58,740 --> 00:09:00,805
so they sell land and shares to

244
00:09:00,805 --> 00:09:02,180
pay their debts.

245
00:09:02,260 --> 00:09:04,600
That pushes prices down again.

246
00:09:04,770 --> 00:09:06,934
The same machine that built the bubble

247
00:09:06,934 --> 00:09:08,977
now takes it apart.

248
00:09:09,217 --> 00:09:11,696
The researchers describe what came next as

249
00:09:11,696 --> 00:09:13,903
two decades of deflation,

250
00:09:13,903 --> 00:09:14,870
weak markets,

251
00:09:14,870 --> 00:09:18,197
and a lost generation of young people.

252
00:09:18,467 --> 00:09:20,357
Some of the biggest names did not

253
00:09:20,357 --> 00:09:21,377
survive.

254
00:09:21,427 --> 00:09:23,259
Yamaichi Securities,

255
00:09:23,259 --> 00:09:25,809
once the fourth largest securities firm in

256
00:09:25,809 --> 00:09:26,481
Japan,

257
00:09:26,481 --> 00:09:28,336
was bankrupt by the end of the

258
00:09:28,336 --> 00:09:29,399
decade.

259
00:09:29,650 --> 00:09:32,048
And the stock market waited,

260
00:09:32,048 --> 00:09:33,232
and waited,

261
00:09:33,232 --> 00:09:35,349
for its old record.

262
00:09:40,983 --> 00:09:43,263
How long does it take to recover

263
00:09:43,263 --> 00:09:45,384
from a bubble this big?

264
00:09:49,983 --> 00:09:51,890
On February twenty-second,

265
00:09:51,890 --> 00:09:53,154
twenty twenty-four,

266
00:09:53,154 --> 00:09:55,940
the Nikkei finally closed above its record

267
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:58,683
from December nineteen eighty-nine.

268
00:09:58,933 --> 00:10:01,813
It had taken more than thirty-four years.

269
00:10:01,863 --> 00:10:05,199
A whole generation of Japanese savers had

270
00:10:05,199 --> 00:10:07,344
grown up and grown old without ever

271
00:10:07,344 --> 00:10:09,250
seeing the stock market as high as

272
00:10:09,250 --> 00:10:10,803
their parents had.

273
00:10:11,050 --> 00:10:11,691
Today,

274
00:10:11,691 --> 00:10:14,343
nobody thinks the Imperial Palace is worth

275
00:10:14,343 --> 00:10:16,490
as much as California.

276
00:10:16,580 --> 00:10:18,942
But the comparison survives because it

277
00:10:18,942 --> 00:10:20,920
captures something real.

278
00:10:21,140 --> 00:10:23,548
When cheap money meets something people

279
00:10:23,548 --> 00:10:25,451
believe can never lose value,

280
00:10:25,451 --> 00:10:27,859
prices stop measuring what a thing is

281
00:10:27,859 --> 00:10:28,700
worth.

282
00:10:28,870 --> 00:10:31,046
They start measuring how much people can

283
00:10:31,046 --> 00:10:32,081
borrow.

284
00:10:32,317 --> 00:10:35,132
So what is the lesson of Japan's

285
00:10:35,132 --> 00:10:36,357
bubble?

286
00:10:36,527 --> 00:10:38,277
That a price can be real on

287
00:10:38,277 --> 00:10:41,377
paper and impossible in practice.

288
00:10:41,647 --> 00:10:43,886
Japan's land was never going to be

289
00:10:43,886 --> 00:10:46,227
sold all at once to anyone.

290
00:10:46,467 --> 00:10:48,675
Its value only existed while everyone kept

291
00:10:48,675 --> 00:10:49,338
believing,

292
00:10:49,338 --> 00:10:50,567
and kept borrowing.

293
00:10:50,567 --> 00:10:53,147
